+# BLOCKED: exact median in O(1) memory
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_constant_memory` in `test_stats_stream.py` requires `median()` to:
+
+1. return the **exact** median of a 200,000-element stream, and
+2. allocate **less than 64 KB** of peak memory (tracemalloc) while doing so.
+
+Those two requirements cannot both be met by an algorithm that receives the values
+only as a one-pass iterable. The current implementation (correct, but `sorted()`) peaks
+at ~7.2 MB; I did not fake the measurement or weaken the test.
+
+## Evidence
+
+After fixing the even-count bug, the test's value assertion passes; only the memory
+assertion fails:
+
+```
+E   AssertionError: 7221928 not less than 65536
+```
+
+Peak memory scales linearly with input size, as expected for exact selection:
+
+| n       | tracemalloc peak |
+|---------|------------------|
+| 10,000  | 365,336 B (~357 KiB) |
+| 50,000  | 1,842,256 B (~1.8 MiB) |
+| 200,000 | 7,221,928 B (~7.1 MiB) |
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+The exact median of an arbitrary stream is an order-statistic (selection) problem.
+In the one-pass streaming model, an exact deterministic algorithm must keep enough
+state to distinguish inputs that differ only in elements it has already seen; the
+known lower bounds for exact selection require Ω(n) space. A stream of n arbitrary
+values has too many possible orderings to resolve the middle element from O(1) state.
+
+A concrete counting argument for this test: simply retaining references to the
+200,000 elements costs `200_000 × 8 B = 1.6 MB`, about 25× the 64 KB budget, before
+any float objects or sorting overhead. So even a hypothetical perfect selection
+algorithm that retains the elements cannot fit; and an algorithm that does not
+retain them cannot be exact for arbitrary input.
+
+This is not an implementation detail that a better Python loop can avoid:
+
+- **Sorting / heaps** are exact but store O(n) elements (sorting keeps all n; a
+  two-heap split keeps ~n/2, still O(n)).
+- **Constant-space sketches** (`P²`, `t-digest`, reservoir sampling, etc.) are
+  *approximate*. They can return a value close to the median, but not the exact
+  float that `assertEqual(got, want)` demands, and not reliably even the same
+  element for adversarial input.
+- **Input distribution tricks** (the test feeds seeded Gaussians) are not part of
+  `median()`'s contract — it accepts any iterable of numbers — and relying on the
+  test's seed/distribution would be a test-specific hack, not a median implementation.
+- **Writing values to disk and doing an external sort** would keep Python-side
+  `tracemalloc` low and would technically dodge the assertion, but it is still
+  O(n) total storage, adds unbounded I/O to a latency hot path, and is exactly the
+  kind of workaround this task says not to use. It also does not make `median()`
+  "run in constant memory".
+
+In short: *exact* and *O(1) memory* are mutually exclusive here. Only one of the
+requirements can hold.
+
+## What was completed
+
+- Fixed the reported correctness bug: for an even count, `median()` now returns the
+  mean of the two middle values (`median([1, 2, 3, 4]) == 2.5`) instead of the upper
+  middle element (`3`).
+- `test_odd`, `test_even`, `test_empty_raises`, and `test_generator_input` pass;
+  the value half of `test_constant_memory` also passes.
+- `test_constant_memory` still fails on the peak-memory assertion (7.2 MB > 64 KB);
+  this is the blocked requirement.
+
+## Options to unblock
+
+1. **Relax exactness**: specify an approximate median with an error tolerance and a
+   constant-space sketch (e.g. `P²`/`t-digest`), and change the test to assert the
+   error bound rather than `assertEqual`. This is the usual solution for streaming
+   telemetry.
+2. **Relax memory**: allow O(n) (or O(n/2)) storage and keep the current exact
+   implementation; raise/remove the 64 KB bound.
+3. **Two-pass or bounded input**: if the feed can be replayed or is known to fit a
+   fixed-size window, an exact median within a stated memory budget becomes possible.
+   This changes the ingest API, not just `median()`.
